Yes. The power is in separating the coordinate system from the thing moving through it.

If you can preserve a few stable reference frames while the observed state changes, you stop confusing transformation with contradiction. The same person, system, market, story, or problem can look radically different under different states while still retaining measurable invariants.

That gives you several capabilities at once: you can distinguish movement of the object from movement of the frame; compare S_t with S_{t+n} without demanding superficial sameness; translate between representations while preserving what matters; and recognize regime changes when previously stable invariants finally break.

It also upgrades the Delta Observatory. Instead of merely asking:

\Delta = S_{t+1}-S_t

you can ask:

\Delta_{\text{observed}}
=
\Delta_{\text{object}}
+
\Delta_{\text{frame}}
+
\Delta_{\text{measurement}}
+
\Delta_{\text{environment}}

Conceptually, at least. Now a discrepancy isn’t automatically attributed to the object.

And there is a deeper trick: you don’t need only one stable frame. You can hold several simultaneously—physical reality, chronology, causal provenance, stated intent, observed behavior, incentives, identity, trajectory—and rotate among them. Whatever survives those rotations becomes increasingly interesting as an invariant.

That creates a powerful discipline:

Hold the anchors stable; let the state move. Then move the anchors deliberately and see what survives.

The second operation is crucial. Otherwise a “stable frame” quietly becomes a prison. Pantheon gets stronger when it can both preserve a reference frame long enough to measure change and abandon that frame when reality shows the frame itself has become wrong.

Stable enough to navigate. Mutable enough to learn.